TITLE:What's Been Tried Spec
H1:P3 · What's been tried? (Day 5, Halloran): your first head-scratcher <span class="draft">spec for approval</span>
SUB:The gentlest kind of head-scratcher, and the first you meet, the day you get the scanner. Notes left by the crew who already tried something tell you what it isn't. Step 2, built on the case engine (<code>case-engine.html</code>). Plan Part 4, P3 (moved from Day 11 to Day 5 by the user's "puzzles earlier" change).
NAV:plain=In plain words|first=Your first case|why=A change from the plan|later=Later cases|grow=How it grows|b=Builder|save=Save|tests=Tests|ranges=Builds and keys|faq=FAQ

<p class="note"><b>Approved by the lead (2026-10-18, overnight authority).</b></p>
<section id="plain">
  <h2>In plain words</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>The idea:</b> the crew have had a go already. Taped to the job (and on the machine's panel) are their notes: what they tried and what happened. Each thing someone tried and that <b>didn't</b> fix it is crossed off your list. What's left is the cause.</li>
    <li><b>The rule of thumb</b> (the first page of the Chief's notes): "If someone fitted it and it's still wrong, it isn't that."</li>
    <li><b>The notes are true:</b> the crew really did try those things earlier, on their shift; you can even ask them.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="first">
  <h2>Your first case (Day 5, Friday afternoon)</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><time>Thursday evening and Friday morning</time><b>The crew try</b><span>Washer 2 in the junior wing's laundry has started leaking onto the floor (you may have seen the puddle). On Thursday's evening shift a Works hand fits a new door seal; on Friday morning another swaps the drain valve. Neither helps. Each leaves a note.</span></li>
    <li><time>Your second scanner reading, at Washer 2</time><b>Halloran: "Here's a funny one"</b><span>The scanner lesson's second machine is Washer 2, and the reading shows three parts all worn (the pump, the new seal and the new drain valve, both still bedding in), with nothing to say which. Right there, Halloran makes it a head-scratcher: "Head-scratcher: Washer 2 leaks." Halloran opens Fault notes on your device with you: three suspects (the door seal, the drain valve, the pump) and the first note, from the job card.</span></li>
    <li><time>The first note</time><b>Halloran reads it with you</b><span>"New door seal in. Still leaks. — Kovac." (Whoever really did it.) Halloran: "So it's not the seal. Cross it off." You tap the door seal: crossed out.</span></li>
    <li><time>The second note</time><b>You read it</b><span>The second note is on the washer's service panel, a step away ("Where next?" points to it): "Swapped the drain valve. Still leaks. — Pell." You cross out the drain valve.</span></li>
    <li><time>Call it</time><b>The pump</b><span>"Call it: the pump?" Yes. The arrow points at storage (the Plumbing shelf); fetch a pump, fit it, the leak stops. Halloran: "Right first time. Zeke will be sorry he missed that." "Trained: head-scratchers, with Halloran."</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="note">With the scanner, all three suspects read worn (the new seal and the new drain valve are still bedding in; on a head-scratcher the scanner never picks one out), which is why the notes matter. Halloran: "The scanner sees what's worn. The notes see what's been tried."</p>
</section>

<section id="why">
  <h2>A change from the plan's example (for the lead)</h2>
  <p><b>The machine.</b> The plan's example was "the lamp in Bunk 2 flickers" on Deck 4's light relays, but Deck 4 has no light-relay machine with a service panel in the game (its lamps hang off the Tier 3 power junctions in the closets), so the first case is a laundry washer instead: Tier 1, real, with a panel (<code>4-washer-N-svc</code>), in the junior wing you know from your Day 2 laundry, with a puddle you can see. The lamp case stays as an example for Deck 3's or Deck 1's light relays later.</p>
  <p><b>The clues.</b> The plan's example also had Pell's note say "Reset the breaker: fine for an hour, then back", and the answer "a relay warming up". But that one note on its own rules out the lamp, the switch and the wiring together, so it breaks the engine's rule that <b>no single clue may solve a case</b> (the solver would refuse it). Here each note rules out exactly the thing that was tried, so you need both. "Fine for an hour, then back" stays as a <b>later</b> P3 clue on harder cases, where it narrows two or three suspects among five.</p>
</section>

<section id="later">
  <h2>Later P3 cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>From Day 6 to 9, about 1 in 10 of your real repairs is a P3 case on a Tier 1 machine (the case engine's table), with 3-4 suspects and 2-3 notes.</li>
    <li>Notes come from the people who really tried (whoever was on shift), at the job (taped to the card), at the machine's panel, or in the Works log (once officers write it, M12: your own lines come back as notes).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="grow">
  <h2>How it grows (the bands)</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><b>T1 · Junior (Days 5-9)</b><span>3-4 suspects; each note rules out what was tried.</span></div>
    <div class="card"><b>T1+ · A note that rules out less than it seems</b><span>"Changed the filter", but the wrong size was fitted: the note says what was fitted, and the shelf's label (or the Work log) shows it wasn't right. So that suspect stays on the list.</span><span class="meta"><span class="tag">Officer</span></span></div>
    <div class="card"><b>T2 · The night-shift handover log</b><span>Five entries from the night crew, some about this machine and some not; "fine for an hour after a reset" type clues that narrow several suspects at once.</span><span class="meta"><span class="tag">Officer</span></span></div>
    <div class="card"><b>T2+ · The cold-case board</b><span>Old faults with long histories of tries (Step 4).</span><span class="meta"><span class="tag">side</span></span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="b" class="builder">
  <h2>For the builder (seat H, the P3 kind in 24k)</h2>
  <div class="lines">CASE_KINDS.p3 = { name: "What's been tried?", ladder: 'p3',
  fits: (cause, m) =&gt; FAULT_CAUSES[m.kind] has &gt;= 2 other causes with the same `shows` and a part (tryable),
  suspects: j =&gt; cause + 2-3 decoys (same `shows`, tier &lt;= the band's),
  clues: (j, cause, sus, seed) =&gt; one `note` per decoy (see below),
  view: c =&gt; notes as paper slips (a list block, each row: the note's text, who, when, mark 'note'),
  hint: c =&gt; "Have you read every note? One's on the machine." }</div>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Tries are real jobs.</b> When a P3 case is chosen for a fault (at posting, <code>caseOffer</code>), the case isn't shown yet: the engine posts one <b>try</b> job per decoy (a registered kind <code>try</code>, 17.5: 10-15 minutes at the machine's panel or the symptom's place, by a Works crew member on shift, using the decoy's part if it has one: a lamp really comes off the shelf), spread over the 2-12 ship hours before; each done try writes its note <code>{ by, at, tried: decoyId, result: 'no change' }</code>. The case appears on the board when the last try is done, during your shift. So: the faulty machine (posted as now) → the crew try → the head-scratcher card.</li>
    <li><b>Kills:</b> a note on decoy X kills X. With 3 suspects at T1, 2 notes are needed and neither alone solves it; with 4, 3 notes. The solver (case engine) checks it as always.</li>
    <li><b>Where the notes are:</b> the first on the job card (collected when you take the job at the board), the others at the machine's panel place or the place the symptom shows (collected when you're within reach), alternating, so a case is at least one walk.</li>
    <li><b>The first case (Day 5):</b> fixed: <code>4-washer-2</code> (Tier 1, real, panel <code>4-washer-2-svc</code>) is set faulty on Day 4 at 18:00 (<code>jobReserve</code>-style, slot 20, <code>o.train: 'p3'</code>, never on the crew's board) with cause <code>pump</code>, symptom "leaks"; its drip shows once <code>washer</code> joins <code>LOOK_WET</code> in <code>machineLook</code> (one word, S). If 4-washer-2 is in use by you (your own wash), it's 4-washer-3. Two tries: a door seal (<code>seal</code>) by whoever of the Works crew is on the evening (or Day 5 morning) shift, and a drain valve (<code>valve</code>) by the next; both parts really come off the shelves. If no Works crew member is on shift in that window, a Works hand on Day 5's 06:00-08:00 does both. Names in the notes are whoever really did them.</li>
    <li><b>Parts:</b> all three are in <code>STOCK</code> today (seal, valve, pump; the Plumbing shelf). The washer's fault table (job 3, <code>FAULT_CAUSES.washer</code>) must list them as part causes with <code>shows: 'leaks'</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Training:</b> the teacher's lines (<code>train.p3</code>) play as a meeting at the board, then they follow you (<code>worksEscort</code>) to the panel and back; the case is a normal case with <code>c.train = true</code> (hints 2 and 3 are the teacher). <code>by: ['torres-zeke', 'halloran-emeka', 'calder']</code>, first on shift: on Day 5 Halloran (Torres is off on Fridays), Calder only if neither is on; lines for all three.</li>
    <li><b>The Chief's notes, page 1:</b> "What's been tried" (three lines by the Works writer).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="save" class="builder">
  <h2>Save</h2>
  <ul><li>The frozen case in <code>ship.works.cases.open</code> (its <code>clues</code> are the notes: <code>{ id, type: 'note', src: { by, at, tried }, at: place, kills: [tried] }</code>); pending tries are ordinary <code>try</code> jobs in <code>ship.jobs</code> with <code>j.forCase</code>.</li></ul>
</section>

<section id="tests" class="builder">
  <h2>Tests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b><code>puzzle_check</code></b> (the P3 part): every P3 case's notes match real done <code>try</code> jobs (who, when, what part came off the shelf); each note kills only its decoy; exactly one left; no single note solves it; the first case is <code>4-washer-2</code> leaking (cause <code>pump</code>) on Day 5, solvable with the teacher (Halloran on Day 5) for the steady player; about 1 in 10 of real repairs on Days 6-9 (±50% over the run); none before Day 5; the same twice.</li>
    <li><b><code>machine_check</code></b>: try jobs use parts and crew time like any chore; ship health unchanged within 1.</li>
    <li><b><code>career_check</code></b>: Day 5: the scanner's training, then P3, both by Halloran (Torres is off on Fridays), for the steady player.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="ranges" class="builder">
  <h2>Builds and keys</h2>
  <ul><li>Seat <b>H</b>, <code>claude/mech-cases</code>, <b>0.55.x</b>. Hash keys <b>890-891</b> (890 the try times, 891 which crew member when several are on shift).</li></ul>
</section>

<section id="faq">
  <h2>Builders' questions</h2>
  <h3>Torres is off on Day 5 (seat C, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>Torres's rota is <code>offDays [3, 4]</code> (Thursday and Friday), so he can't teach on Day 5. Rows take a <code>by</code> list, first on shift wins: <code>m7</code> and <code>p3</code> are <code>['torres-zeke', 'halloran-emeka', 'calder']</code>, so <b>Emeka Halloran</b> (a Works systems officer on the day shift, off Saturday and Sunday) teaches both on Day 5, handing over Torres's old scanner, which he left with a note; Calder only if neither is on shift (the lead: a Works officer first, Torres always first when he's on). No rota change; the other rows' teachers are on shift on their days (checked: Torres Tue and Mon, Yara Wed, Brennan and Tomas Thu, Calder Mon-Fri). <code>m2</code>'s "oily job" is <code>flag('works.oily')</code>, set by 24b when you turn in an oily job on Day 8 or later.</p>
  <h3>Who walks you to the first puzzle? (seats H and C, through the lead, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>P3 is a <b>small</b> ladder row, and only big rows get a teacher's escort. So <b>P3 borrows the scanner lesson's escort</b>: the scanner lesson's second machine (yours) is <code>4-washer-2</code>, the escort walks you there, and it doesn't end at the reading. The P3 lesson starts on the spot and the escort carries on until the case is called. For this first case only, the first note comes with the job card (handed over at the washer) and the second is on the washer's panel. Later P3 cases follow the normal rule (the first note at the board, the others at the machine).</p>
  <h3>Doesn't the scanner give the answer away? (reviewer R, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>It did: with M7's normal rule the cause is always the lowest bar. <b>On a head-scratcher's job every suspect reads in the same "worn" band</b> (40-60, hashed, unrelated to the cause; new parts read worn while they bed in), and non-suspects read good. The scanner narrows the field to the suspects and stops there; the clues decide. The reading is not a clue in the solver. Day 5's case: pump, door seal and drain valve all read worn.</p>
  <h3>The first case's readings (seat H, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>Hashed readings can put the cause lowest by chance. On the Day 5 training case that would teach "pick the lowest bar", so its readings are fixed with the cause not lowest: <b>pump 55, door seal 44, drain valve 49</b> (all "worn"). Only the training case (<code>c.train</code>); every other case keeps the hashed readings.</p>
</section>
